SPEC = {'file': 'cover_xapien_ai_quality_engineer', 'label': 'Xapien AI Quality Engineer', 'center': True, 'paras': ['I am writing to apply for the AI Quality Engineer position at Xapien in London. I am completing an MSc in Health Data Science at University College London, where a distinction is expected, and I currently work as a Data Analyst at TikTok Shop UK.', "The part of this role I am closest to is LLM evaluation. As an Applied AI Engineer at DS Digital Technology, I worked on a medical AI engagement for a pharmaceutical client. Before building anything, I agreed with the client's clinical experts what a safe answer looked like and turned that into a 246-question benchmark and a six-dimension rubric with red-line rules, which the client adopted as its acceptance standard for patient-facing AI answers. I then built a Python pipeline that called six LLM APIs with retrieval and search augmentation, produced and scored over 2,400 responses with an LLM-as-judge framework, and ran blinded A/B tests to check whether a medically reviewed knowledge graph actually improved answer quality, rather than assuming it would.", 'That work taught me the point you make in the posting: a score is only useful if you question it. In my MSc dissertation (Health Data Science, UCL), when some results came out close to zero, I treated them as something to investigate rather than accept, and the investigation uncovered a missing dataset that made the results more robust. My statistics background, including hypothesis and permutation testing, calibration and causal inference, helps me judge whether a metric has really moved.', 'At TikTok Shop UK I am a Data Analyst. I built an AI agent with Claude Code, with a validation step to check its output, and I explain metrics to operations, product and commercial teams.', 'The honest gap is test automation: I have not yet built Playwright or pytest suites or owned CI/CD. I learn quickly under pressure, for example moving from a biology degree to building LLM evaluation pipelines for a client, and I would be glad to come in through your second door.', "Thank you for considering my application. I would welcome the opportunity to discuss how my experience designing and running LLM evaluations could contribute to Xapien's quality work."]}
